---
description: General development guidelines on code style, testing, and documentation.
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---
 • Look for an `AGENTS.md` file for package level context. Use grep to absorb all AGENTS.md files.
 • Create an use a `CONTEXT.md` file for your own purposes which you may update with information about development plans, progress, analysis, or anything else you wish to save for memory/context reasons. It is your file to control.
 • Kismet is in early development and nothing needs to remain for backwards compatibility or version changing. Do not leave legacy system code around, clean up after refactors.
 • Use lowerCamelCase for struct fields and JSON tags. Do not use snake_case.
 • Constants must be in UPPER_CASE and may use underscores.
 • Avoid global state. Prefer dependency injection via constructor functions.
 • Prefer composition over inheritance. Use interfaces for abstractions, not for struct reuse.
 • Always check errors returned by functions. Handle explicitly or propagate upward.
 • Use `gofmt` and `golangci-lint`.
 • Use explicit type declarations for map and slice initializations.
 • Avoid naked returns. Return named values explicitly.
 • Limit function size. Split functions into smaller units when they exceed 50 lines or contain deeply nested blocks.
 • Avoid panic in business logic. Only use for programmer errors or unrecoverable internal states.
 • Use context.Context consistently in all long-running or external-facing functions.
 • Prefer immutable data patterns for concurrent data (channels, sync primitives).
 • Use 4-space indentation. Do not use tabs.
 • Use parentheses to clarify operator precedence in math or bitwise operations.
 • Avoid single-letter variable names outside loop counters or math domains.
 • Use meaningful, descriptive identifiers. Avoid obj, data, temp.
 • Booleans should start with passive verbs (e.g., isActive, hasPermission).
 • Arrays must be pluralized (users, not user[]).
 • Naming conventions:
   • lowerCamelCase → fields, parameters, JSON keys
   • UpperPascalCase → exported types, structs, functions
   • UPPER_CASE → constants
   • Use kebab-case for filenames, folders, and case-insensitive identifiers.
 • Insert whitespace lines to separate variable declarations, return statements, and major logic blocks.
 • Avoid magic numbers. Define constants for reusable numeric values.
 • Use build tags appropriately for platform or environment-specific logic.
 • All publicly exposed functions, types, and packages must be documented with GoDoc comments.
 • Package-level docs must describe the role of the package.
 • Write unit tests for every exported function and type.
 • Aim for at least 70% test coverage.
 • Use table-driven tests with descriptive case names.
 • For probabilistic logic, use fixed seeds for reproducibility.
 • Design types and functions to support future extensions like orthography and cultural links.
 • Keep abstractions clean: avoid premature optimization. Prioritize clarity and extensibility.
 • When generating new code, also add or change unit tests relevant for the new code.
 • Each package should contain a root `package.go` file with package level documentation applied.